An Intranet Explorer :)
=======================

Fragd may be used to fragment streams of IP packets.
You must set up a proper environment before. I assume you know
how to set up a router on linux or BSD. Disable routing
(i.e. echo 0 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward on linux) and start
fragd with apropriate devices. Modify offsets[] array
in fragd.cc for your needs. Default is that packets
are splitted into 16-byte plus rest packet. Resulting datagram
is then sent in two framgments in 'right' order. You maybe want
to send second first, then turn on '-r' switch.

The syntax for offsets[] is as follows:

{OFFSET[1], LENGTH[1], OFFSET[2], LENGTH[2], ..., OFFSET[N], -2, -1}

where OFFSET[X] means that data in reassembly starts at 8*OFFSET[X]
(as 'fragmentation offset' is defined in the RFC). LENGTH[X] is
the length of the data-portion. '-2' is a special length which
tells fragd to choose the rest of the length. '-1' means that
there is no more data. In general we have that 

LENGTH[N-1] = 8*OFFSET[N]

If your array doesn't fit into this formula there's something
wrong and the result isn't defined (or you generate overlapping fragments:)
Note that all sane firewalls forbid tiny-fragments (offset 0, length 8),
so you'd need to patch kernel to fire this one through.
Also you need to set the constant of 'maxoff' which is just the maximum
offset that can occur during iterate through offsets[]. This allows fragmentation
routine to discard fragmentation of packets which can't.
Intranet explorer was tested on Linux. BSD should work too.

Fragd maybe also used to test IDS or to bypass 'em.
When IDS check for common overflow attacks, they use patterns recorded
in their rule-files. However some IDS think that TCP-segments must have PUSH flag
enabled. This is not true. Fragd allows you to clear this flag before forwarding and
thus not triggering the IDS.

fraggle
-------

Fraggle is the FreeBSD version which can be used with even just one normal
computer (not GW). That is you set up special firewall rule via

ipfw add divert 10000 ip from <my-host-ip> to any

which will redirect raw packets to "fraggle". Start fraggle on this port, and all
outgoing traffic will be scrambled (as by fragd).

fraggle -p 10000

Make sure that you enabled DIVERT etc in your kernel.
Also make sure that this rule is applied first (before any other rules).

I want to point out that dugsong wrote "fragrouter" which maybe
fits better into your needs. Check out www.monkey.org/~dugsong.

Sebastian
